00:15:34,981 --> 00:15:36,440
that I think I captured.

244
00:15:37,420 --> 00:15:39,720
But sketching the coin is just the first step.

245
00:15:40,660 --> 00:15:42,120
Next, he's sculpting it.

246
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:43,700
And that's tricky business.

247
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:47,160
The design has to be almost entirely flat,

248
00:15:47,300 --> 00:15:49,780
so the finished coin is as streamlined as possible.

249
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:51,860
This is such low relief.

250
00:15:52,380 --> 00:15:55,200
Our maximum is 80 thousandths of an inch,

251
00:15:55,240 --> 00:15:56,660
and that's as high as we can go.

252
00:15:56,720 --> 00:15:58,080
We have to work within those parameters.

253
00:15:58,081 --> 00:16:02,120
80 thousandths of an inch is about as thin as a sheet of paper.

254
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:06,760
Creating depth while working within that range is just a bit challenging.

255
00:16:07,400 --> 00:16:10,460
When you do a sculpture like this, it's really a lot of illusion.

256
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:13,740
You have to do what I call painting with light,

257
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:18,160
where you're sculpting planes and angles to catch light and to catch shadow

258
00:16:18,161 --> 00:16:21,660
so that you create a dynamic-looking portrait in this case.

259
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:26,360
One of the final touches on any coin is a minuscule mark

260
00:16:26,361 --> 00:16:30,620
that goes unnoticed by most people, the artist's initials.

261
00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:32,420
They're hard to see in the actual coin,

262
00:16:32,540 --> 00:16:33,380
but you know they're there.

263
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:36,720
If another artist designs it and I sculpt it,

264
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:38,740
the other artist's initials would go on the left

265
00:16:38,741 --> 00:16:39,740
and mine would go on the right.

266
00:16:39,820 --> 00:16:41,540
In this case, I designed and sculpted it

267
00:16:41,541 --> 00:16:43,520
so my initials would go on the right side.

268
00:16:44,860 --> 00:16:45,860
Once it's finished,

269
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:48,220
the model is handed over to the tooling team.

270
00:16:48,980 --> 00:16:51,020
They're in charge of making the hub and die,

271
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:54,240
two crucial pieces necessary for coining.

272
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:58,840
This steel cylinder will become what's called a reduction hub.

273
00:16:59,380 --> 00:17:03,200
It's essentially a coin-sized metal replica of the artist's model.

274
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:09,400
The reduction hub will be mechanically etched over 12 hours.

275
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:14,940
Once complete, the reduction hub must be painstakingly cleaned.

276
00:17:16,140 --> 00:17:17,680
You have to be careful.

277
00:17:18,320 --> 00:17:21,520
If you are a little bit too overzealous, you'll ruin it.

278
00:17:21,521 --> 00:17:25,520
So it's a very meticulously controlled bit of work

279
00:17:25,521 --> 00:17:28,520
where you are using little pieces of stone,

280
00:17:28,521 --> 00:17:31,520
sanding pads, things like that, to get rid of the lines.

281
00:17:31,521 --> 00:17:33,520
The tiniest stray lines,

282
00:17:33,521 --> 00:17:37,520
or even the slightest speck of dust, will ruin the dyes.

283
00:17:37,521 --> 00:17:39,520
From this, which is a positive,

284
00:17:39,521 --> 00:17:41,520
they will be able to make a series of dyes,

285
00:17:41,521 --> 00:17:43,520
which are negatives, and the negatives will be used

286
00:17:43,521 --> 00:17:49,520
to actually stamp the coins that will wind up in your pocket.

287
00:17:49,521 --> 00:17:53,520
Once clean, the hub is placed in the die press.

288
00:17:53,521 --> 00:17:58,520
This machine centers the reduction hub over a cone-shaped cylinder.

289
00:17:58,521 --> 00:18:03,520
A press descends, flattening the hub and cylinder together.

290
00:18:03,521 --> 00:18:08,520
The etched image of Abraham Lincoln is forced onto the steel cylinder.

291
00:18:08,521 --> 00:18:12,520
What emerges from the die press is the master die,

292
00:18:12,521 --> 00:18:16,520
which has a recessed, rather than raised, image.

293
00:18:16,521 --> 00:18:22,280
After even more cleaning, the die is ready for work on the production line, where it

294
00:18:22,281 --> 00:18:28,820
will stamp out millions of shiny new United States coins.

295
00:18:28,821 --> 00:18:33,880
Each one of those coins begins as a 1,500-foot-long metal coil.

296
00:18:33,881 --> 00:18:39,740
This coil is pure copper, sandwiched between layers of manganese brass.

297
00:18:39,741 --> 00:18:45,440
The coils are unrolled and fed through a blanking press, which punches out round disks, called

298
00:18:45,441 --> 00:18:46,230
blanks.

299
00:18:46,231 --> 00:18:55,600
The leftover metal, or webbing, gets melted down and recycled, while the new blanks

300
00:18:55,601 --> 00:19:00,568
head to an annealing furnace for a heat treatment.

301
00:19:00,569 --> 00:19:05,040
This alters the physical makeup of the metal,

302
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:09,431
softening its surface in preparation for coining.

303
00:19:09,432 --> 00:19:12,800
After washing and drying, the blanks are run

304
00:19:12,801 --> 00:19:18,172
through an upsetting mill. This machine raises a rim around the edges.

305
00:19:18,173 --> 00:19:19,860
The mill consists of a

306
00:19:19,861 --> 00:19:22,284
rotating wheel with a groove on its edge.

307
00:19:22,285 --> 00:19:25,300
The grooved edge of the wheel is fitted to a curved

308
00:19:25,301 --> 00:19:29,768
section called a shoe, which has a corresponding groove.

309
00:19:29,769 --> 00:19:32,800
The distance between the wheel and the

310
00:19:32,801 --> 00:19:34,897
shoe gets progressively narrower.

311
00:19:34,898 --> 00:19:38,900
As the blank is rolled along the groove, a raised rim is formed

312
00:19:38,901 --> 00:19:42,040
on both sides.

313
00:19:42,041 --> 00:19:45,040
Finally, the blanks are ready for striking.

314
00:19:45,041 --> 00:19:48,140
This happens in the coining press,

315
00:19:48,141 --> 00:19:53,640
where master dies stamp out 12 new coins every second.

316
00:19:53,641 --> 00:19:56,360
This intense striking process means the dies

317
00:19:56,361 --> 00:19:58,960
require constant replacing.

318
00:19:58,961 --> 00:20:02,560
They're under such pressure when they strike a coin.

319
00:20:02,561 --> 00:20:04,360
For example, to make a dollar coin,

320
00:20:04,361 --> 00:20:07,500
it takes about 80 tons of pressure.

321
00:20:07,501 --> 00:20:10,300
We make 12 coins per second.

322
00:20:10,680 --> 00:20:15,380
So those guys are hitting together using 80 tons of pressure 12 times a second.

323
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:29,460
Every batch of new coins is carefully inspected for the slightest defect that could indicate a worn-out die.

324
00:20:29,720 --> 00:20:35,440
On the side of every coining press, there's a trap, and it takes about 10 minutes to fill that trap.

325
00:20:35,441 --> 00:20:41,000
And once it's filled, the operator's going to take the last two coins that were struck

326
00:20:41,001 --> 00:20:44,820
and are going to look at them to make sure that they're the best quality that can be made.

327
00:20:45,300 --> 00:20:49,940
Any imperfections means the entire batch of coins is sent to the recycling bin.

328
00:20:51,040 --> 00:20:53,880
Every coin has to be exactly alike.

329
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:55,320
Every coin has to be perfect.

330
00:20:56,160 --> 00:20:59,980
If they're not, you're going to try and put them into a vending machine and they're not going to work.

331
00:21:00,620 --> 00:21:02,900
Plus, they're the coins of the United States.

332
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:04,660
So we want them to be perfect.

333
00:21:04,661 --> 00:21:07,220
We're proud to be making a perfect product here.

334
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:16,360
Perfect batches are sent to the counting machines,

335
00:21:16,980 --> 00:21:18,380
where they're bagged,

336
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:21,280
weighed,

337
00:21:21,820 --> 00:21:26,180
and sent out into the world.

338
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:29,700
These new coins will end up any number of places,

339
00:21:29,701 --> 00:21:39,600
But one of the most captivating just happens to be 164 feet below the Earth's surface in one of the most lucrative wishing wells on the planet.

340
00:21:42,440 --> 00:21:45,760
Coming up, what do they do with all those coins?

341
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:55,680
The Lincoln Memorial Penny is the most common circulating coin.

342
00:21:55,681 --> 00:21:59,580
A close look at its reverse side reveals a surprising feature.

343
00:22:00,260 --> 00:22:03,880
Inside the memorial is a minuscule statue of Abraham Lincoln.

344
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:08,260
It's a mirror image of the real Lincoln Memorial on the National Mall.

345
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:12,300
More Coin Operated will return on Modern Marvels.

346
00:22:13,060 --> 00:22:17,240
We now return to More Coin Operated on Modern Marvels.

347
00:22:18,900 --> 00:22:22,280
Throughout a coin's estimated 25-year lifespan,

348
00:22:22,281 --> 00:22:26,280
it's almost guaranteed to travel to some unexpected places.

349
00:22:26,281 --> 00:22:31,280
Like wishing wells, it's estimated that worldwide,

350
00:22:31,281 --> 00:22:34,280
people toss more than a million pounds of coins

351
00:22:34,281 --> 00:22:37,280
into wishing wells each year.

352
00:22:37,281 --> 00:22:40,280
Most of this gets donated to charity,

353
00:22:40,281 --> 00:22:43,280
which means the coins have to come out of the well.

354
00:22:43,281 --> 00:22:47,280
It's not a terribly difficult task,

355
00:22:47,281 --> 00:22:52,280
unless the wishing well is 164 feet below the Earth's surface.

356
00:22:52,281 --> 00:22:56,280
Luray Caverns in Virginia's Shenandoah Valley

357
00:22:56,281 --> 00:22:59,280
is more than 400 million years old.

358
00:22:59,281 --> 00:23:03,280
It's the most popular cave system in the eastern U.S.

359
00:23:03,281 --> 00:23:07,280
And since 1954, one of the main attractions here

360
00:23:07,281 --> 00:23:10,280
has been this natural pool.

361
00:23:10,281 --> 00:23:15,280
It's one of the most lucrative wishing wells on the planet.

362
00:23:15,281 --> 00:23:17,280
One, two, three.

363
00:23:17,281 --> 00:23:18,420
One, two, three.

364
00:23:18,421 --> 00:23:19,420
Okay.

365
00:23:19,421 --> 00:23:20,880
Yeah.

366
00:23:24,780 --> 00:23:27,520
We're talking about a lot of coins.

367
00:23:27,521 --> 00:23:32,520
Precisely since 1954, 36,072,131 coins,

368
00:23:36,040 --> 00:23:41,040
amounting to over 134 tons of money donated to charity.

369
00:23:42,600 --> 00:23:45,980
A grand total of almost a million dollars.

370
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:50,880
Collecting all these coins is not an easy job.

371
00:23:50,980 --> 00:23:54,920
The well is six feet deep, the deepest pool in the caverns.

372
00:23:55,020 --> 00:23:57,720
Wading in is not an option.

373
00:23:57,820 --> 00:24:01,820
Instead, the entire well has to be drained once a year

374
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:05,040
by Luray's maintenance staff.

375
00:24:05,140 --> 00:24:07,340
We're getting ready to pump the water out

376
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:09,340
of the wishing well.

377
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:13,380
And we do this annually to collect all of the coins.

378
00:24:13,480 --> 00:24:14,880
OK, you get the hose.

379
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:24,280
It's a two-day process, and it all begins by lowering a sump pump down into the pool.

380
00:24:24,281 --> 00:24:30,280
This is the same type of pump homeowners often use to remove water from flooded basements.

381
00:24:30,281 --> 00:24:33,280
Okay, I'm gonna plug the pump up.

382
00:24:33,281 --> 00:24:34,280
Okay.

383
00:24:34,281 --> 00:24:35,280
There we go.

384
00:24:35,281 --> 00:24:41,280
The sump pump will take water out of the wishing well and store it in an adjacent pool.

385
00:24:41,281 --> 00:24:46,280
Later, once the coins are collected, workers will refill the well.

386
00:24:46,281 --> 00:24:52,500
The water in this pool, as in any of the pools in the caverns, is seepage.

387
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:57,880
And that is from water that just drips down from the surface over a long period of time.

388
00:24:58,600 --> 00:25:05,460
Now, if we were to leave this water out and let it seep back in, naturally, it would take a very long time.

389
00:25:06,140 --> 00:25:09,320
But by pumping this back, we may lose a little bit,

390
00:25:09,321 --> 00:25:15,600
but the seepage from the ground above will fill it back up to its normal height.

391
00:25:16,280 --> 00:25:21,580
Once the sump pump starts working, it will take eight hours to drain the pool.

392
00:25:28,800 --> 00:25:34,000
The next morning, a gold mine of treasure awaits Luray's maintenance staff.

393
00:25:36,740 --> 00:25:43,120
Workers armed with burlap sacks, shovels, and wheelbarrows survey the massive piles of coins.

394
00:25:43,121 --> 00:25:50,120
In some areas, the coins have accumulated in mounds more than two feet deep.

395
00:25:50,121 --> 00:25:58,120
Right now we're cleaning the coins down off the walls, putting them into the lowest point,

396
00:25:58,121 --> 00:26:04,120
and from there we'll shovel them into buckets, put the buckets of coins into bags, about

397
00:26:04,121 --> 00:26:09,120
200 bags on average per year.

398
00:26:09,121 --> 00:26:14,120
There's an estimated $30,000 to $40,000 in this batch,

399
00:26:14,220 --> 00:26:17,120
which will all go to several different charities

400
00:26:17,220 --> 00:26:19,120
designated by the town of Leray.

401
00:26:20,140 --> 00:26:23,140
Within our small community in Leray,

402
00:26:23,240 --> 00:26:25,140
there is a secret committee that is assembled

403
00:26:25,240 --> 00:26:28,100
that determines the amounts and the recipients

404
00:26:28,200 --> 00:26:30,140
of these contributions.

405
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:44,120
It takes the team about four hours to clear the pool,

406
00:26:44,121 --> 00:26:47,120
but the job is far from done.

407
00:26:47,121 --> 00:26:51,120
Now the crew has to get thousands of pounds of coins

408
00:26:51,121 --> 00:26:53,120
out of the cavern.

409
00:26:53,121 --> 00:26:57,120
The wishing well is about a half a mile from the exit,

410
00:26:57,121 --> 00:27:00,120
and with barrels that weigh about 200 pounds apiece,

411
00:27:00,121 --> 00:27:04,120
teamwork's definitely a big factor.

412
00:27:04,121 --> 00:27:08,120
It's a long, winding journey out of the cavern.

413
00:27:08,121 --> 00:27:11,960
of the cavern, and the crew begins a wheelbarrow relay.

414
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:29,280
At the end of the path, there's one final obstacle.

415
00:27:29,380 --> 00:27:32,380
It's a workout worthy of the local high school football

416
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:33,180
team.

417
00:27:33,181 --> 00:27:42,480
Every year, the boys volunteer their time to help haul sacks of coins up several flights

418
00:27:42,481 --> 00:27:43,480
of stairs.

419
00:27:43,481 --> 00:27:48,760
It's a good workout for a good cause.

420
00:27:48,761 --> 00:27:52,420
Over the years, some of the national health organizations that have received donations

421
00:27:52,421 --> 00:27:57,160
from the Loray Caverns Wishing Well are the American Cancer Society, the March of Dimes,

422
00:27:57,161 --> 00:28:02,120
the Juvenile Diabetes Research Foundation, among many others.

423
00:28:02,121 --> 00:28:07,120
between $30,000 and $40,000 is taken from this pool of water each year.

424
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:12,120
But the coins have been sitting in water for a year.

425
00:28:14,120 --> 00:28:18,120
Many of them are corroded and covered with slime.

426
00:28:18,121 --> 00:28:22,120
In other words, this is dirty money that no bank will touch.

427
00:28:24,120 --> 00:28:29,120
But one enterprising company has figured out a way to turn that toxic treasure...

428
00:28:29,121 --> 00:28:31,120
Sweet Penny! Treasure! Treasure!

429
00:28:31,121 --> 00:28:37,141
cold hard cash. It's a surprising job most of us never knew existed.

430
00:28:37,142 --> 00:28:39,000
That's next on Modern

431
00:28:39,001 --> 00:28:46,919
Marvels. We now return to more coin operating on Modern Marvels.

432
00:28:46,920 --> 00:28:50,260
What do you do with $30,000

433
00:28:50,261 --> 00:28:57,766
worth of slimy coins that came from the bottom of a wishing well? Just ask this guy.

434
00:28:57,767 --> 00:28:58,660
Walt McKay

435
00:28:58,661 --> 00:29:02,660
The company of Dallas, Texas, has created a thriving business

436
00:29:02,760 --> 00:29:04,460
out of dirty money.

437
00:29:04,560 --> 00:29:08,100
Walt owns FountainCoins.com, a small company

438
00:29:08,200 --> 00:29:11,240
with a valuable mission, to turn those grimy fountain

439
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:16,180
coins into something bankable.

440
00:29:16,280 --> 00:29:19,820
Well, when we get the coins, usually they've

441
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:21,520
been sitting around for a while.

442
00:29:21,620 --> 00:29:24,580
And at the bottom of the bucket, the stuff that came out

443
00:29:24,680 --> 00:29:27,060
of the bottom of the fountain or the water feature

444
00:29:27,061 --> 00:29:28,560
It's pretty grimy.

445
00:29:28,561 --> 00:29:31,200
By the time we dump it out and get a nice whiff of it,

446
00:29:31,201 --> 00:29:32,760
it's pretty ripe.

447
00:29:33,960 --> 00:29:38,200
Walt has been cleaning fountain coins since 2003.

448
00:29:38,201 --> 00:29:41,500
It may sound like an easy task, but coming up with a workable

449
00:29:41,501 --> 00:29:44,380
method was a lesson in trial and error.

450
00:29:44,381 --> 00:29:48,920
I broke our clothes washer by putting coins in a bag and

451
00:29:48,921 --> 00:29:50,080
tumbling around in there.

452
00:29:50,081 --> 00:29:52,420
I had to repair that myself.

453
00:29:52,421 --> 00:29:56,320
I broke our dishwasher pretty much the exact same way.

454
00:29:56,321 --> 00:30:00,320
Today, Walt uses a small cement mixer and an assortment of chemicals.

455
00:30:01,020 --> 00:30:04,500
Not a very high-tech process, but it gets the job done.

456
00:30:05,580 --> 00:30:10,700
First thing we do is dump all these dirty coins in a cement mixer,

457
00:30:11,060 --> 00:30:17,060
and we rinse them several times so we get the floating debris

458
00:30:17,061 --> 00:30:20,200
and the majority of the dirt off of it.

459
00:30:20,660 --> 00:30:23,040
But dirt isn't the only thing Walt finds.

460
00:30:23,880 --> 00:30:26,180
It's just a world of treasure in here.

461
00:30:26,320 --> 00:30:29,120
There's my lunchtime toothpick.

462
00:30:29,121 --> 00:30:32,860
Marbles, broken jewelry pieces.

463
00:30:32,861 --> 00:30:36,860
Little bit of caulking from the fountain there.

464
00:30:36,861 --> 00:30:39,360
Ooh.

465
00:30:39,361 --> 00:30:41,860
That would be juicy fruit.

466
00:30:50,380 --> 00:30:52,280
You can see how all the stuff that floats

467
00:30:52,281 --> 00:30:54,180
is already starting to come off.

468
00:30:54,181 --> 00:30:58,080
It's a nice, sweet, soupy mix of the stuff

469
00:30:58,180 --> 00:30:59,480
we're trying to get out.

470
00:30:59,580 --> 00:31:01,080
After the initial rinse,

471
00:31:01,180 --> 00:31:03,780
Walt has to get rid of some of the organic material,

472
00:31:03,880 --> 00:31:06,820
slime, algae, or moss that collects on the coins.

473
00:31:06,920 --> 00:31:12,140
And just a splash of this chlorine is all it takes.

474
00:31:16,240 --> 00:31:20,440
We'll let it work its magic for a little while.

475
00:31:20,540 --> 00:31:22,480
Oh, the water's already turning lighter.

476
00:31:22,580 --> 00:31:23,780
Take a look at it.

477
00:31:23,781 --> 00:31:25,780
Get it?

478
00:31:25,781 --> 00:31:27,780
Good.

479
00:31:27,781 --> 00:31:31,780
This water's just like water that would be in a swimming pool at this point.

480
00:31:31,781 --> 00:31:34,780
Next, a dash of laundry detergent.

481
00:31:34,781 --> 00:31:38,780
This will cut through any remaining grease and grime.

482
00:31:38,781 --> 00:31:42,780
Looks like my hot tub at home after a big party.

483
00:31:44,780 --> 00:31:50,780
After a few minutes, the coins are ready for the next addition, an abrasive.

484
00:31:50,781 --> 00:31:59,540
The reason we need the abrasive is some of these have so much scale or calcification

485
00:31:59,541 --> 00:32:03,020
that they won't even fit through a commercial coin counter.

486
00:32:03,920 --> 00:32:07,120
Our mission here is to make this penny worth a penny.

487
00:32:11,500 --> 00:32:16,420
This is our own secret sauce from Texas Hook'em.

488
00:32:16,421 --> 00:32:23,140
It'll take about two hours for the abrasive to do its job.

489
00:32:25,960 --> 00:32:29,440
But when it's finished, these coins look a lot different.

490
00:32:30,180 --> 00:32:32,840
You can see how much cleaner the coins are at this point.

491
00:32:33,160 --> 00:32:38,980
There's no scale, and there's nothing to stop it from going through the chute at the bank.

492
00:32:40,240 --> 00:32:44,100
An acid wash shines them up and gets rid of any lingering debris.

493
00:32:44,101 --> 00:32:49,100
Then the batch is dried and spread out to be sorted.

494
00:32:49,101 --> 00:32:53,100
Dangerous items are tossed.

495
00:32:53,101 --> 00:32:55,100
This is the torture.

496
00:32:55,101 --> 00:32:57,100
Luckily, it didn't stick me,

497
00:32:57,101 --> 00:33:00,100
but it goes into the torture box here.

498
00:33:00,101 --> 00:33:02,100
Foreign coins are saved.

499
00:33:02,101 --> 00:33:05,100
Oh, good. Okay, we got a euro, five euro cent.

500
00:33:05,101 --> 00:33:07,100
What else do we have?

501
00:33:07,101 --> 00:33:08,100
And good old Mexico.

502
00:33:08,101 --> 00:33:11,100
Texas is close to Mexico, so I have a lot of pesos.

503
00:33:11,101 --> 00:33:15,580
And every so often, Walt finds something really exciting.

504
00:33:15,940 --> 00:33:18,540
Wheat penny, treasure, treasure, punch the button.

505
00:33:19,280 --> 00:33:20,360
There we go.

506
00:33:20,900 --> 00:33:21,600
All right.

507
00:33:21,840 --> 00:33:24,540
It's fun when we find old coins, the wheat pennies.

508
00:33:25,960 --> 00:33:30,540
Wheaties were the regular U.S. penny between 1909 and 1958.

509
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:34,560
They're popular among modern collectors because they're old,

510
00:33:34,840 --> 00:33:39,700
but also because they're almost entirely copper, unlike new pennies.

511
00:33:39,701 --> 00:33:47,280
The new pennies for many years have been zinc with a copper plating, and the copper

512
00:33:47,281 --> 00:33:54,940
plating is relatively thin, and it gets eaten through, so we have to pick out all these

513
00:33:54,941 --> 00:33:57,960
pennies, and the ones we try to make, you know, it's still, you're only talking about

514
00:33:57,961 --> 00:33:59,000
one cent at a time.

515
00:33:59,140 --> 00:34:01,000
It's the hardest part of the job.

516
00:34:01,840 --> 00:34:06,780
A penny won't buy much, but the biggest problem with it is this surprising fact.

517
00:34:06,781 --> 00:34:09,600
It costs more than a cent to make a cent.

518
00:34:09,700 --> 00:34:13,700
Zinc and copper have skyrocketed in price.

519
00:34:13,701 --> 00:34:16,700
It does cost more than a penny to make a penny these days.

520
00:34:16,701 --> 00:34:19,700
It depends upon the metal market of the month

521
00:34:19,701 --> 00:34:22,700
and how much we can purchase the metal for each month.

522
00:34:22,701 --> 00:34:27,700
But by and large, it's a cent and a half to 1.7 cents right now,

523
00:34:27,701 --> 00:34:30,700
that's public information, of how much it costs us to make a penny.

524
00:34:30,701 --> 00:34:34,700
The economy of that may not make much sense,

525
00:34:34,701 --> 00:34:38,700
but the penny is the most widely used coin in the U.S.

526
00:34:38,701 --> 00:34:42,960
Us Americans are very emotionally attached to our penny,

527
00:34:43,180 --> 00:34:44,860
and there's two sides of the argument.

528
00:34:45,200 --> 00:34:48,020
If we should continue the penny, we shouldn't continue the penny.

529
00:34:48,800 --> 00:34:54,400
At least for now, most people, as well as Congress, want the penny to stay around.

530
00:34:54,900 --> 00:34:59,000
And that's probably a good thing, because even cents add up.

531
00:34:59,460 --> 00:35:02,080
We want to count every single penny that we can,

532
00:35:02,180 --> 00:35:05,360
because all the proceeds of these coins go to charities.

533
00:35:05,361 --> 00:35:08,860
After sorting through the entire batch of coins,

534
00:35:08,861 --> 00:35:11,860
Walt bundles them up and puts them through a counting machine.

535
00:35:11,861 --> 00:35:14,860
It makes a neat sound. I love the sound.

536
00:35:14,861 --> 00:35:18,860
Every little click is another wish.

537
00:35:18,861 --> 00:35:21,860
We put about $50 worth into a bag like this.

538
00:35:21,861 --> 00:35:23,860
It weighs 31 pounds.

539
00:35:23,861 --> 00:35:27,860
I like to keep track of the weight of the coins,

540
00:35:27,861 --> 00:35:31,860
and I can tell how much debris and trash we've removed

541
00:35:31,861 --> 00:35:34,860
from the original gross weight.

542
00:35:34,861 --> 00:35:35,920
I have a net weight.

543
00:35:37,060 --> 00:35:39,460
The coins will be sent to the bank for a recount,

544
00:35:39,980 --> 00:35:42,160
and Walt will take a small cut for his troubles.

545
00:35:43,280 --> 00:35:45,520
A check for the rest is sent to the customer.

546
00:35:46,320 --> 00:35:49,740
I have a form that I send every customer.

547
00:35:50,200 --> 00:35:53,200
It gives a precise count of every penny,

548
00:35:53,600 --> 00:35:56,440
every nickel, dime, quarter, whatever.

549
00:35:56,960 --> 00:35:58,840
I take the time to look through

550
00:35:58,841 --> 00:36:00,700
because that's what I like to do.

551
00:36:02,420 --> 00:36:04,820
These coins will go back out into the world

552
00:36:04,821 --> 00:36:09,840
where many of them will undoubtedly find their way into a coin-operated machine.

553
00:36:10,840 --> 00:36:13,700
What comes out is anybody's guess.

554
00:36:14,420 --> 00:36:17,620
Coming up, coin-operated comes alive.

555
00:36:22,340 --> 00:36:28,340
In 1943, pennies were struck in steel because of wartime copper shortages.

556
00:36:28,860 --> 00:36:34,140
The steel cent is the only coin issued by the U.S. that contains no copper.

557
00:36:34,141 --> 00:36:38,120
More Coin Operated will return on Modern Marvels.

558
00:36:38,460 --> 00:36:42,680
We now return to more Coin Operated on Modern Marvels.

559
00:36:43,920 --> 00:36:47,960
The Coin Operated industry provides some useful services.

560
00:36:49,480 --> 00:36:51,020
Take the car wash.

561
00:36:51,540 --> 00:36:54,940
It's the most popular and convenient way to wash our cars.

562
00:36:55,400 --> 00:37:00,820
So it was just a matter of time before someone decided to shrink it down to doggy size.

563
00:37:00,821 --> 00:37:05,640
Here in Hoboken, New Jersey, Coin Operated has a new leash on life.

564
00:37:06,120 --> 00:37:11,120
This is the K9000, a Coin Operated shower for our four-legged friends.

565
00:37:11,440 --> 00:37:15,320
On the weekends, we could do up to $60 to $70.

566
00:37:15,960 --> 00:37:16,700
Rufus, come.

567
00:37:19,720 --> 00:37:23,220
Just insert money, and with a simple flip of a switch,

568
00:37:23,680 --> 00:37:27,180
you can shampoo, rinse, even condition.

569
00:37:28,180 --> 00:37:31,880
Hidden inside the machine are all natural organic products

570
00:37:31,881 --> 00:37:35,620
and a water heater that allows customers to choose their own temperature.

571
00:37:35,860 --> 00:37:36,680
Say hello to America.

572
00:37:38,240 --> 00:37:40,760
A flea and tick rinse is the final coat,

573
00:37:41,100 --> 00:37:43,640
and a blow dry tops it all off.

574
00:37:45,080 --> 00:37:48,760
$15 for small dogs, $20 for large ones.

575
00:37:49,760 --> 00:37:51,880
And whether you have four legs or two,

576
00:37:52,160 --> 00:37:55,660
the K9000 takes convenience to a whole new level.

577
00:37:55,661 --> 00:37:57,320
The phrase I get the most is,

578
00:37:57,321 --> 00:37:59,660
I'll never ever wash my dog at home ever again.

579
00:38:02,700 --> 00:38:04,940
Efficient self-service is the cornerstone

580
00:38:04,941 --> 00:38:06,560
of the coin-operative business.

581
00:38:07,760 --> 00:38:09,560
And when it comes to vending,

582
00:38:09,561 --> 00:38:13,660
self-service is a $300 billion industry.

583
00:38:13,661 --> 00:38:17,180
Every day, 100 million Americans insert money

584
00:38:17,181 --> 00:38:19,480
into one of seven million vending machines

585
00:38:19,481 --> 00:38:21,560
scattered across the country.

586
00:38:21,561 --> 00:38:25,320
We buy everything, from sodas and snacks

587
00:38:25,321 --> 00:38:26,780
to aspirin and ice cream.

588
00:38:28,260 --> 00:38:31,820
But this machine pushes the limits of traditional vending.

589
00:38:31,821 --> 00:38:34,660
It's called the 24-hour bait shop,

590
00:38:34,661 --> 00:38:38,940
and it caters to fishermen who tend to keep odd hours.

591
00:38:38,941 --> 00:38:43,440
People love them because they can get their products 24-7.

592
00:38:43,441 --> 00:38:46,200
The bait shops don't have to be open.

593
00:38:46,201 --> 00:38:48,840
You can go there at four o'clock in the morning,

594
00:38:48,841 --> 00:38:51,420
10 o'clock at night, any time of day or night,

595
00:38:51,421 --> 00:38:54,940
get a full wide array of bait and tackle.

596
00:38:54,941 --> 00:38:59,700
The temperature-controlled machine is stocked with creatures only an outdoorsman could love.

597
00:39:00,320 --> 00:39:07,720
Minnows, worms, even crayfish, all perfectly alive in their own biodegradable containers.

598
00:39:08,620 --> 00:39:15,120
It's just not your typical bag of potato chips or your bottle of soda or can of soda.

599
00:39:15,300 --> 00:39:19,980
We have all different size packaging, a wide array of things that have to go in there.

600
00:39:20,640 --> 00:39:24,360
The only thing we can't vend, we haven't figured out how to vend, is a rod.

601
00:39:24,940 --> 00:39:29,100
the 24-hour bait shop takes vending to a new level of sophistication.

602
00:39:30,620 --> 00:39:36,460
Just like this machine, the Wine Kiosk, is a massive high-tech vending machine that

603
00:39:36,461 --> 00:39:42,860
recently hit the scene in Pennsylvania, where by law, wine is only sold in state-owned stores.

604
00:39:44,620 --> 00:39:48,940
What this kiosk is going to do is bring the convenience of purchasing wine and spirits to

605
00:39:48,941 --> 00:39:51,198
customers who are shopping within this grocery store.

606
00:39:51,199 --> 00:39:52,860
So essentially, we've just brought that

607
00:39:52,861 --> 00:39:55,980
to them as opposed to having them go out to purchase the wine and spirits.

608
00:39:56,940 --> 00:40:00,940
The process is simple. Just choose a bottle on the machine's touch screen,

609
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:06,140
insert a driver's license, and smile for the camera.

610
00:40:06,141 --> 00:40:09,740
On the other side of this camera, there's actually a live individual who

611
00:40:09,741 --> 00:40:13,260
watches and documents every single transaction that has occurred.

612
00:40:13,980 --> 00:40:18,060
A state employee remotely approves the sale after verifying all information

613
00:40:18,860 --> 00:40:20,540
and ensuring the buyer isn't drunk.

614
00:40:20,541 --> 00:40:27,540
Please take a deep breath and blow firmly into the breath alcohol sensor as shown in the video.

615
00:40:27,541 --> 00:40:33,540
Once a payment is made, the kiosk locates the wine and allows the buyer to take their bottle.

616
00:40:33,541 --> 00:40:36,540
The bottles are in the cabinets indicated by the flashing image.

617
00:40:36,541 --> 00:40:41,540
Essentially what we've tried to do is keep the experience of buying wine that customers are used to.

618
00:40:41,541 --> 00:40:46,540
So they're not looking at a vending purchase, so that when they walk up to the wine they can browse the wine,

619
00:40:46,541 --> 00:40:48,540
they can look and see which wine they want.

620
00:40:48,541 --> 00:40:50,540
They can also, when they purchase the wine,

621
00:40:50,541 --> 00:40:53,540
they're actually opening the door and taking the bottle out,

622
00:40:53,541 --> 00:40:55,540
just like they would in a store.

623
00:40:55,541 --> 00:40:57,540
So essentially, we've kept the experience,

624
00:40:57,541 --> 00:40:59,540
but have allowed them to do it in a self-service manner.

625
00:40:59,541 --> 00:41:01,540
Thank you for your purchase.

626
00:41:01,541 --> 00:41:06,540
The wine kiosk uses the latest in vending technology,

627
00:41:06,541 --> 00:41:09,540
a sure sign that the coin-operated industry

628
00:41:09,541 --> 00:41:13,540
is quickly evolving into a highly mechanized business

629
00:41:13,541 --> 00:41:17,040
that sells products too pricey for just a few coins.

630
00:41:18,640 --> 00:41:20,140
But for one company,

631
00:41:20,700 --> 00:41:24,180
a few coins has been a successful business model for half a century.

632
00:41:25,960 --> 00:41:28,600
Seacoast Manufacturing in Fairhope, Alabama,

633
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:32,080
designs and manufactures classic coin-operated viewers

634
00:41:32,081 --> 00:41:37,260
that have given us up-close looks at iconic places since 1960.

635
00:41:37,740 --> 00:41:41,620
You put your money in, you wind it up, and you have a view.

636
00:41:41,621 --> 00:41:44,920
You don't vend anything.

637
00:41:45,200 --> 00:41:48,200
You're vending a view of something that's already there.

638
00:41:49,960 --> 00:41:51,500
It's a simple concept,

639
00:41:51,860 --> 00:41:54,600
but these telescopes and binoculars are tough,

640
00:41:55,160 --> 00:41:58,340
built to withstand almost anything, even hurricanes.

641
00:41:58,860 --> 00:42:01,700
I remember a time that one had been underwater

642
00:42:01,701 --> 00:42:04,240
after a hurricane for two weeks,

643
00:42:04,780 --> 00:42:06,780
and in a matter of two hours,

644
00:42:07,220 --> 00:42:09,980
Dad and I cleaned it up, and it was back working.

645
00:42:09,981 --> 00:42:13,397
The secret to this incredible durability?

646
00:42:13,398 --> 00:42:16,980
Stainless steel, aluminum and brass bodies.

647
00:42:16,981 --> 00:42:23,980
And a complete lack of electronics. No batteries, no wires, no power adapters.

648
00:42:23,981 --> 00:42:28,980
Just a simple mechanism that works like a wind-up clock.

649
00:42:28,981 --> 00:42:33,980
When coins are inserted, they trigger a timing mechanism that flips open a shutter.

650
00:42:33,981 --> 00:42:39,260
shutter. The timing mechanism holds the shutter open for a minute and a half and

651
00:42:39,261 --> 00:42:43,700
then flips it back over the eyepiece.

652
00:42:44,080 --> 00:42:49,740
What's great about these machines is that they are made to withstand the

653
00:42:49,741 --> 00:42:55,740
elements, you know, extreme heat, extreme cold, very little maintenance. These

654
00:42:55,741 --> 00:43:00,140
machines are built to last using time-tested techniques that have changed

655
00:43:00,141 --> 00:43:03,140
changed very little in half a century.

656
00:43:07,640 --> 00:43:12,980
The only change since 1960 is that you had to put a dime in it

657
00:43:12,981 --> 00:43:15,680
and now you have to put 50 cents in it.

658
00:43:15,681 --> 00:43:18,100
The only change is change.

659
00:43:21,300 --> 00:43:23,400
Today, for just two quarters,

660
00:43:23,401 --> 00:43:27,800
anyone can enjoy an up-close view of the natural world,

661
00:43:27,801 --> 00:43:29,039
the man-made one.

662
00:43:29,040 --> 00:43:34,580
It's a coin-operated beast for the eyes, until your minute and a half is up.

663
00:43:35,520 --> 00:43:40,400
And when that happens, there will always be somewhere else to drop those extra coins.

664
00:43:42,920 --> 00:43:48,600
Because even in a world that's becoming less dependent on small change, those pennies,

665
00:43:48,601 --> 00:43:54,964
dimes, nickels, quarters, and Lincoln dollars can always go into a piggy bank.

666
00:43:54,965 --> 00:43:56,840
That's just good sense.

667
00:43:58,120 --> 00:43:59,500
Thank you.

